{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>When you approach, etc.</b> Perhaps this section alludes, according to what the RShBI said in the Zohar (vol. 2, 62a), that the Master, blessed be He, sends an additional soul to a person from the supernal world to guide him on the straight path, and through its mediation a person is saved from sin. And HaShem commanded the soul and said: <b>when you approach a city</b> — this is the human body, which is called a ״city,״ as they said in the Zohar (Zohar Ḥadash, Ruth 97a) on the verse ״a little city״ (Ecclesiastes 9:14), and this is its language: ״a little city״ — this is the body of the son of man, end quote — <b>to make war</b>: for strength is needed to save the person from the hand of the one who would make him sin." "<b>And it is precise</b> to say ״upon it״ — meaning, for the sake of the city, to save it from the evil inclination, in the manner of the verse (Ecclesiastes 9:14) ״and came against it,״ etc., and he saved the city through his wisdom (Nedarim 32b)." "<b>And you shall call to it for peace.</b> Meaning, that you should not come to it with your strides to incline it toward the good path from one extreme to the other, but rather at the outset, as you approach, you shall call to it for peace — that what he does he should do also for the supernal world, and he will benefit from this in this world and in the world to come, and he shall allocate a portion to physicality and a portion to spirituality. For a person needs provisions also for the supernal world, and if he does not exert himself in this world on behalf of the world to come as well, it turns out that he will not attain it and will be lost forever. And this path is called ״words of peace,״ for it is effective." "And the verse's saying <b>and it shall be, if it answers you with peace.</b> The word ״and it shall be״ (והיה) is an expression of joy — it is a joy if it answers you with peace. And it says <b>and it opens</b> — meaning, no more than an opening is needed, and if he opens [himself] like the eye of a needle, the Holy One, blessed be He, will open for him like the opening of a hall. And this is what the verse concludes to say: <b>and all the people found in it</b> — 248 limbs and 365 sinews, all of them shall be subjugated to the soul: both in the aspect of positive commandments, they shall be subject to perform them with their bodies and their property, etc.; and in the aspect of negative commandments — <b>and they shall serve you</b> — meaning, like a servant who fears his master, so as not to deviate from his words right or left." "And the verse's saying <b>but if it does not make peace, etc.</b> Meaning, if the city did not make peace and turned its back from the path. And sometimes it happens that after a person has begun to improve his deeds he turns backward, and when this has happened to a person, his wickedness grows great and his inclination strengthens against him more than at first, to wage war with the soul. And this is what the verse says: <b>and it makes war against you.</b> HaShem will command to besiege the city with fasts and afflictions, as they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): a person should always agitate the good inclination against the evil inclination — and the agitation is to subject oneself through things that are permitted to him, also through fasts and lashes and weeping, to wear down the power of evil that is in the city. And HaShem promises him to deliver it into his hand, as the verse says: ״and HaShem your Elohim will deliver it into your hand.״ <b>And you shall strike every male in it</b> — this is the power of ס״מ and his hosts." "And the verse's saying <b>by the sword</b> — this is in the manner of what is said (Psalms 149:6): ״רוֹמְמוֹת אֵל בִּגְרוֹנָם וְחֶרֶב פִּיפִיּוֹת בְּיָדָם״." "And the verse's saying <b>only the women and the little children</b> — meaning: ״women״ are nefesh and ruach, and they are called women in the aspect of the neshamah, as is known to those who know grace. ״And the little children״ are the commandments and good deeds that a person performed before he was detestable in the eyes of HaShem, in the sod of (Psalms 50:16) ״וְלָרָשָׁע אָמַר אֱלֹהִים מַה לְּךָ לְסַפֵּר חֻקָּי״, and when a person repents they are reckoned to his merit. Also according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said (Yoma 86b) that inadvertent sins become for him like merits, and these are called טף." "And the verse's saying <b>and the beast</b> refers to the desire of the material, which is called behemah, <b>and all that is in the city shall be spoils for you</b> — meaning that everything shall be for the benefit of the soul, to repair what needs repair for the attainment of intelligibles, for even the bestial desire can be adapted to a good disposition in whatever pertains to it." "<b>And the verse's saying: thus you shall do to all the cities, etc.</b> The meaning is that all the commandments stated in this matter apply only to the cities — which are the bodies that have drawn far away, in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 57:19) ״לָרָחוֹק וְלַקָּרוֹב״ — for a transgressor is called far from the Shekhinah, which is the source of holiness from which the supernal soul is hewn. But a city that is of the cities of the nations — meaning, one that has no connection with holiness and has departed from its source through the multitude of its transgressions like all the nations — the soul has no portion with it and no hope of restoring it. And this is what the verse says: <b>which are not of the cities of those nations</b>, etc., for those aspects — nothing is to be done with them except to place them under the ban. And this was the error of Moshe in accepting the erev rav (Shemot Rabbah 42), and HaShem said to him that their root is evil and they do not stand within the category of holiness but are set apart for the evil portion. And when he would give them life — meaning, bring them near to holiness — he does not give life to every soul in them. Note precisely the word ״כָּל״ — this is the ruach, which is the first to be defiled along with the neshamah:" "<b>Thus concludes the parashah of Shofetim</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:42.191390946Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}